A||||3||Cooter||????? For WebMasters||||1||
Z||000000||Cooter||10-11-2000||09:41 PM||||I am updating my school's website (again  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> ) and was wondering how to convert all my jpg pictures into a faster loading format. Thanks-||||reg||1
Z||000001||Songman||10-12-2000||12:22 AM||||jpg is the fastest loading format for the net. The main this is to get the resolution down. Most people scan at 300 because they have heard that number. If you are scanning for a website, convert your scans to 72dpi. That is all a standard monitor is good for anyway.<P>There are other things you can do like with palettes and stuff like that, but that can get tricky.<P>Get a newer copy of Photoshop or Imageready. They have built-in options to save for web. My sites are all graphic intensive and load very quickly.<P>E-mail me if you need help (or software).<p>[This message has been edited by Songman (edited 10-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tigger||10-12-2000||01:26 AM||||Cooter,<P>Yep, reduce the resolution to 75 like Songman said.<P>I use Micrographix Picture Publisher 8.  It works great and you can preview the quality vs. file size of your images on the fly as you save them.  Many high end effects and features come with it too for a reasonable price compared to other graphics programs.<P>You can also try downloading <A HREF="http://www.jasc.com" TARGET=_blank>Paint Shop Pro</A>.  It's a 30 day trial, works great and has pretty good options as well.<P>If your stuck with whatever your using, then try adjusting the compression levels for your files.  Usually most programs have Low, Med, High compression settings under the "options" dialog or in a list box when you save them.  You might be able to try using the Med. setting to reduce the size of the images for a small tradeoff in color quality that nobody will notice.  It's kind of trial and error.<P>.jpg's are great for photo type pictures like from a scanned photograph with a lot of colors.  Compressing .jpgs kind of makes them fuzzy and the colors blend scattered like.<P>.gif's seem to work great for line art type pictures like cartoons.  When photographs are converted or compressed in this format they tend to create noticable color gradients that don't look so good, however you don't notice the gradients when the picture is made up with just solid colors.<P>------------------<BR>T-T-F-N, Tah Tah For Now!<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/tigger.gif">            <BR>   <A HREF="mailto:RacingStripes@hotmail.com">RacingStripes@hotmail.com</A>  <p>[This message has been edited by Tigger (edited 10-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||10-12-2000||09:34 AM||||It depends on the picture just how much you can chop the compression down wiht JPEG. If you have embeded text you want to read JPEG at the lowest settings can make it really look bad.<P>In one page I'm working on I cut only the compression and droped the total page size to 25-30% of it's starting size. (There's no text I need in any of the images so I could compress them pretty tight.)<P>compression is usually in the save or save as dialogs. To chop an existing picure you usually have to do a "save as" to see the compression selector. (Sometimes you have to click an "advanced" button to get to the compression selector in the save window. depends what program your using.)<P>JPEG is what's called a "losy" compressor. It throws out parts of the image. It's specifically designed to work on photos. It doesn't care about resolution or color depth. Like I said above tho, it can really take a dump on text or similar areas of the picture.<P>GIF98a (it's full name There's also GIF87a which has a few less features.) can be used for photo's but it can have bizar results. GIF is a loseless compressor, it keeps every bit in the image, but only allows 256 colors and 96 DPI resolution. You can use an "optimized pallet" but even that won't prevent weird things happening to photos. GIF really likes any kind of line art image. You can get such images down to incredibly small sizes with GIF plus it won't trash text like JPEG can.<P>One thing GIF89A can do that JPEG can't is set a transparent color. This gives you images where the background shows right up to the edge of a shape. (Like the mail box in my <A HREF="http://home.sprynet.com/~theogre" TARGET=_blank>main cave.</A> that image is actually square. I think the transparrent color on that one was blue.)<P><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-12-2000).]||||reg||1
